n(19, "concept", "The smallest loop that can improve has four parts, and only two of them move the number",
 "Stripped to what the ablations actually support, a self-improving loop is: a worker that produces an artifact; a verifier that turns the artifact into a signal; an archive that keeps more than the latest artifact; and a selector that chooses what to extend or keep. The archive is load-bearing (DGM -27 points without it; Frontis +30 from search alone on held-out; +2.83 from better ranking at equal evaluations). The verifier is load-bearing only in the weak sense that no signal means no gradient (arXiv:2609.34924 argues this structurally but concedes its own ablation did not confirm that diagnostic feedback beats blind resampling). Feedback richness is decorative at measured effect sizes (mode p=0.61). Everything else -- longer runs, more candidates, richer critique, majority voting -- either saturates (Prop. 3) or actively hurts when cost-matched (arXiv:2607.28576: 10 of 36 comparisons reliably worse).",
 "https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.22954; https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.28568; https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29121; https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.34924", "medium", ["synthesis", "minimal-loop", "components"]),

